        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Andreas Dilger",
          "email": "adilger@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-20 16:05:09.000000000",
          "tz": -360
        },
        "subject": "LU-20276 osd-zfs: limit memory of large object frees",
        "message": "LU-20276 osd-zfs: limit memory of large object frees\n\nFreeing a large object in osd_unlinked_object_free() dirties all of\nits L1 indirect blocks.  ZFS limits the frees and dirty data of each\npool separately (zfs_per_txg_dirty_frees_percent, zfs_dirty_data_max),\nso destroying large objects on many OSTs of one OSS at the same time\ncan pin more dirty memory than the node has.  sanityn test_56a/56b\ncreate sparse objects with one L1 block per GiB, ~16K per OST, and\ntheir destroys in the following tests drove a 4GiB OSS with 8 ZFS\nOSTs out of memory in dbuf_dirty(), with ~30K dirty indirect blocks.\n\nEstimate the indirect blocks each free dirties from the object size,\ncounting at most zfs_dirty_data_max for each OSD, and start a free\nonly while the total for all OSDs stays within a quarter of RAM.\nFrees start in arrival order, and an OSD whose pool is suspended\ndoes not hold up the others.  Use zfs_dirty_data_max if ZFS exports\nit, otherwise its default of min(RAM/10, 4GiB).\n\nRemove the sanityn test_56b exception for ZFS servers.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial fstype=zfs testlist=sanityn\nTest-Parameters: fstype=zfs testlist=sanityn env=ONLY=\"56a 56b 70a 70b 71a\",ONLY_REPEAT=10\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5 ltvm llm_code_and_review_tools rainshadow\nChange-Id: I7175701c4a323b20f383486ad0b96d33d93ebbe5\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
